Why this folder exists
======================

python-apt describes a system's software sources from a template named
after the ID of /etc/os-release.  Ours says `ID=raye`, so aptsources
opens /usr/share/python-apt/templates/raye.info.  When that file is not
there, `aptsources.distro.get_distro().get_sources()` raises
NoDistroTemplateException and everything built on it stops before doing
any work: `add-apt-repository`, `remove-apt-repository`,
software-properties-gtk and the repository dialog of Synaptic.

raye-release ships both files, so an upgrade of python-apt cannot take
them away.

Four traps, each one worth a test in release/apt/tests:

1. The file name is the os-release ID, lower case, exactly.  The matcher
   globs `*.info` and uses the base name as the distribution of every
   template it reads; `Distribution.id` is the ID lower-cased, and the
   two have to be equal.

   Measured in the image on 2026-11-05 (python3-apt 3.1.0ubuntu1.1): the
   same file under the name `Raye.info` reproduces the owner's error word
   for word -- "could not find a distribution template for
   raye/resolute" -- because the matcher then registers it under `Raye`.
   A capitalised name (`Ubuntu.info`) was the rule of the old python-apt;
   resolute ships `ubuntu.info`, `debian.info`, `kali.info`, all lower
   case.  `get_distro()` takes the ID straight from os-release and does
   not capitalise anything.

2. No brace in a Suite field.  A braced suite is a template that
   python-apt expands from /usr/share/distro-info/<id>.csv, and
   distro-info-data has no file for us.  Every suite is written out, so
   the CSV is never opened.  A new codename is added by hand.

3. A suite needs a MatchURI, or the matcher drops it and get_sources
   never sees it.  A suite with a ParentSuite inherits the parent's.

4. apt_pkg.TagFile does not know about comments.  A `#` line inside a
   paragraph is read as a field, and its continuation lines swallow the
   real field that follows it: a comment above `Suite:` makes the whole
   suite disappear, with no error anywhere.  Comments live in the leading
   paragraph only, and this README carries the rest.

The suite of the current codename comes first in the file: get_sources
keeps the first template whose name is the codename, and that one has to
be the archive the system installs from, not Raye's own archive.


Two things measured on 2026-11-05 that are not traps in this file, but
that anyone editing it needs to know
====================================================================

`MirrorsFile` widens MatchURI.  DistInfo adds every server of the mirror
list to the suite's match_uri.  The base suite therefore claims
`archive.ubuntu.com` through `raye.mirrors` as well as through its own
`MatchURI`, and changing only the MatchURI does not stop it matching.
That is why the base suite wins the Ubuntu archive line even though
`ubuntu.info` claims the same URI.

Which .info is read first is not decided anywhere.  SourceEntryMatcher
globs `*.info` and does not sort; `match()` keeps the FIRST template
whose MatchURI and suite name fit.  The image's `archive.ubuntu.com` +
`resolute` line fits both this file's base suite and `ubuntu.info`'s
(resolute is in ubuntu.csv, and ubuntu.info is templated from it).
Measured in the image, `raye.info` is read before `ubuntu.info` and
wins; forced the other way round, the repository dialog shows
"Ubuntu 26.04 'Resolute Raccoon'" and "Officially supported" for Raye's
own distribution line.  Nothing breaks -- get_sources still picks this
file's template, because it also matches on the distribution -- but
Ubuntu's and Canonical's wording would appear in Raye's dialog.  Today's
order is luck, not design; `tests/test_apt_template.sh` fails if it ever
flips.

Not about this file, and open: add-apt-repository computes the file it
edits as `<sourceparts>/<ID>.sources`, which is
`/etc/apt/sources.list.d/raye.sources` -- the name raye-archive-keyring
already uses for Raye's own archive.  Measured: `add-apt-repository -c
universe` adds `universe` to the Raye archive, and `-p proposed` creates
`resolute-proposed` on it, instead of touching the base archive in
`ubuntu.sources`; both would 404 on the next `apt update`, and both edit
a file dpkg ships.  Adding or removing a PPA is unaffected (it writes its
own file).  Fixing it is a packaging decision: ship Raye's archive under
another name, or the base archive under this one.
